Inclusion
---------

Register this directory as an Isabelle component before your application
component. Its settings export ISABELLE_MCP_LIBRARY_HOME and
ISABELLE_MCP_LIBRARY_JAR. Your etc/build.props should require:

    requirements = \
      env:ISABELLE_SCALA_JAR \
      env:ISABELLE_MCP_LIBRARY_JAR

Your session ROOT should list MCP-Tool-Library under sessions; import
"MCP-Tool-Library.MCP_Tool_Library" in a Pure theory. For HOL use the
MCP-Tool-Library-HOL session and import
"MCP-Tool-Library-HOL.MCP_Library". The HOL entry point anchors theory data in
HOL and is the safe default for HOL developments. Declare your own tools with
the existing mcp_tool/mcp_codec commands.

The library owns etc/options:mcp_bridge_max_reply_bytes; do not redeclare it
in the application component.

Scala entry points
------------------
isabelle.mcp.McpToolData owns the existing result, schema, annotation and
catalogue types and decoders. McpBridgeOperations constructs tools, runTool
and checkContext operations; McpBridgeProfile.base retains the existing hello
requirements. isabelle.mcp.pide.PideBridge provides the existing blocking call
interface, cancellation port, readiness and shutdown methods.

PideBridge takes PideTransport, host-supplied PideBridgePolicy, a DeadlineScheduler,
a catalogue root selector, operation names, profile, protocol and diagnostics.
SessionPideTransport accepts an isabelle.Session, including Headless.Session and
application-owned PIDE sessions. It uses the base session protocol interface. The
application owns prover startup, a completed/pinned catalogue root document,
readiness, and process shutdown. Call awaitReady after the root is available.
Do not create a second protocol handler for the same result functions in one
session. Drain closes ML admission process-wide and belongs to host shutdown.
The host also installs the catalogue notification handler for MCP.tools_changed
if it needs catalogue-change notifications; the MCP server remains an example.

Invocation routing still uses one pending registry and typed promises. Calls
may run concurrently; completion/cancellation/deadlines use the existing terminal
ownership rules. Cancellation can settle the local result before ML stops. There
is no intermediate response stream, new channel API or new public async API.
The optional arguments.context locator is resolved in ML after catalogue lookup;
omitting it uses the catalogue context. The legacy context parameter in tools
and runTool payloads does not select a different catalogue.

